\date{2026-02-14T17:06:18Z}
\title{Forester setup for Discrete Math lecture notes}
\author{brentyorgey}

\import{discrete-macros}
\import{base-macros}

\p{I spent a bunch of time this week writing [Discrete Math lecture notes](0021) in Forester, and tweaking my Forester setup to make the process more efficient and the results prettier.

\ul{
  \li{I used [Pandoc](https://pandoc.org/) to write a [janky script](XXX) that takes my existing LaTeX lecture notes and makes a first pass at converting them into Forester syntax.  There's still lots more features I could add, and it's nowhere near being a complete, general-purpose pandoc writer (I'm not sure it will ever be), but it already saves me time.}

  \li{After [asking for advice on the forester-discuss mailing list](https://lists.sr.ht/~jonsterling/forester-discuss/<CAH0njdutB1dVSn4gMRHKFyZhgSzVcrr5KmpCj_3PDoRCqSk_Uw@mail.gmail.com>) and getting a helpful response, I created some [simple Forester macros](https://github.com/byorgey/forest/blob/main/trees/sproof.tree) for typesetting calculational proofs, like so:

    \p{
      \stmt{2n - 1 > 7}
      \reason{\iff}{add #{1} to both sides}
      \stmt{2n > 8}
      \reason{\iff}{divide both sides by #{2}}
      \stmt{n > 4}
    }
  }

  \li{When teaching my Discrete Math students to write proofs, I emphasize the way the proofs should be structured, \em{i.e.} nested.  I have not yet gone so far as teaching them via a proof assistant like Lean or Agda—I'm not sure I ever will—but I get as close as possible while still writing proofs on paper.  I spent a bit of time tweaking some CSS to be able to easily typeset things like this:

  \proof{
    Let #{k} be an arbitrary natural number; then we must show #{\Odd(k) \to \Odd(k^2)}.
    \subproof{
      Suppose #{\Odd(k)}, that is, there exists an integer #{j} such that #{k = 2j+1}.  Then we must show that #{k^2} is also odd.
      \subproof{
        Proof
      }
    }
  }

  This works by creating a new taxon called "Subproof" and [using CSS to hide the header and draw the bar on the side](XXX).  I'm not sure if there's a better way to accomplish this, but it works pretty well for now.
  }
}
}
